{"chunk_id": "392ef6522a1d", "text": "In this section Bhishma recounts how the sage Narada, after praising the supreme deity with secret names, is granted a vision of Narayana in his cosmic form. The god appears with countless heads, arms, and colors, chanting the Vedas and embodying the entire universe. Narayana explains that only those wholly devoted can behold him and then expounds a profound cosmology: the relation of Purusha, Prakriti, the three gunas, and the chain of dissolution by which the elements merge back into the supreme reality. He describes his fourfold emanations\u2014Vasudeva, Sankarshana, Pradyumna, and Aniruddha\u2014and declares himself the source, witness, and end of all beings. The deity also foretells many of his incarnations, including the boar, Narasimha, Vamana, Rama, and Krishna, who appear to restore cosmic order. After revealing these mysteries of creation, devotion, and divine manifestation, Narayana disappears, and Narada later recounts the revelation as sacred doctrine.\nNarayana reveals his cosmic form to Narada, explains metaphysics of Purusha and creation, and foretells his many divine incarnations across ages.\nThemes: cosmic theophany, Vedantic cosmology, avatars of Vishnu, devotion and liberation, creation and dissolution", "vector": [0.00992172583937645, -0.009928300976753235, 0.016266634687781334, 0.0066374968737363815, -0.016253484413027763, -0.0014933545608073473, -0.02885124832391739, -0.02476157806813717, -0.01621403358876705, 0.003751056967303157, 0.013768121600151062, 0.017331790179014206, -0.008731645531952381, 0.005075926426798105, -0.0054375529289245605, -0.0013240474509075284, 0.03547888249158859, -0.015517080202698708, 0.024248724803328514, -0.014872726984322071, -0.002424543723464012, 0.003839819924905896, -0.02518237940967083, 0.003915432840585709, -0.01698988862335682, 0.006101631559431553, 0.03334857150912285, -0.03219136595726013, 0.019146498292684555, -0.018331194296479225, -0.002268386771902442, 0.0046025239